23: 
24: GSD is retired in this repository. Never invoke or execute GSD skills, workflows, scripts, or automation, 
including progress, planning, verification, updates, or reinstallation. GSD skills, `.planning/`, and their old 
plans/state are historical reference only; consult them for context or clarification. Their phase status, gates, 
config, and model profiles do not control new work or establish SPEC readiness. Start new work under 
`spec-interview/<slug>/` according to `spec-to-done`; preserve the historical material. This user instruction 
supersedes older GSD guidance and saved memories.
25: 
26: ### Adversarial review gate
27: 
28: Every spec-to-done work item gets an adversarial review at these points:
29: 
30: 1. SPEC: before asking the user to approve it.
31: 2. PLAN: before executing the first task, and after every replan.
32: 3. Block: the PLAN groups tasks into blocks of related work (about 4–5
33:    tasks). After a block's last task, its combined diff is reviewed once.
34:    Tasks are not reviewed one by one; a task is recorded done after its own
35:    light checks (see Verification), with its block review still ahead.
36: 4. Final: the whole work-item diff, before the REPORT.
37: 
38: Keep review rounds few:
39: 
40: - A finding needs another round only when it is High or Medium and a real
41:   user or a realistic failure (network, browser, crash, concurrency) can
42:   trigger it. Low findings are fixed or rejected and recorded, without
43:   another round.
44: - A finding that needs the application's own server to be broken or tampered
45:   with is recorded as a follow-up with its reason, not fixed in the block.
46: - Each block gets at most one fix round after its review. Findings left after
47:   that round are recorded in TRACK and carried to the final review.
48: 
49: The review is important work under Cross-model delegation: a Claude
50: orchestrator runs it through `delegation-anthropic.md`, and an OpenAI
51: orchestrator through `delegation-openai.md`. The review attacks the artifact,
52: not only its conformance to the SPEC: it tries to bypass guards, probes edge
53: and failure inputs, and checks every claim in comments and summaries. Save each
54: prompt and result under `spec-interview/<slug>/reviews/`. Fix or explicitly
55: reject each finding with a reason, and record the verdict and the disposition
56: of every finding in TRACK. A review that fails, times out, or runs with a
57: lower model or effort is unverified, and the gate stays open.
58: 
59: ## Working principles
60: 
61: - Search for existing components, hooks, types, RPCs, and migrations first;
62:   reuse or extend them instead of creating parallel implementations.
63: - Reproduce reported failures before editing, and fix the root cause rather
64:   than the symptom.
65: - Do not suppress errors, bypass type safety, or add unrelated refactors.
80: 4. application deployed against that database.
81: 
82: Report every migration created or edited and whether it was applied. Do not
83: claim the application is ready against a target whose required database
84: contract is missing or unverified.
85: 
86: Before changing an online database, name the target and obtain explicit
87: authorization unless the conversation already authorizes that action and
88: target. Explain backup, rollback, maintenance, and destructive-test implications
89: when relevant. After the change, read back the migration/catalog state from the
90: same target and run a small runtime smoke check.
91: 
92: ## Handoff
93: 
94: Inside spec-to-done, its REPORT format governs the final report. For work
95: outside it, lead with the outcome, then summarize changes, verification, and
96: real remaining limitations or user actions. Small changes need only a short
97: report.
98: 
99: For work involving database or deployment state, report in this order:
100: 
101: 1. Outcome: which affected workflows are usable and which remain unverified.
102: 2. Changes: distinguish application changes, migration/data state on the named
103:    database, and deployment compatibility.
104: 3. Required user actions, if any.
105: 4. Verification: evidence and limits of the checks performed.
106: 5. Unresolved user-visible, database, or rollout risks, if any.
107: 
108: Keep raw logs and orchestration details in TRACK or an optional technical
109: note. Use a pending status only for a specific outstanding step or acceptance.
110: 
111: ## Skills
124: - Browser workflow verification: use the available browser or Playwright skill.
125: - React/Next.js implementation or refactor: use the applicable Vercel skill.
126: 
127: Preserve third-party skills. Apply their relevant guidance through this
128: project's contracts: users.id/supabase_uid identity, canonical Presence
129: authority, and existing TanStack Query/repository boundaries. Generic examples
130: do not replace those contracts. Keep Presence architecture in its canonical
131: skill and host entries as forwarding files.
132: 
133: ## Supabase, authentication, and RLS
134: 
135: - API routes and server code use createSupabaseServerClient from
136:   src/lib/supabase/server-client.ts.
137: - Client Components use createSupabaseBrowserClient from
138:   src/lib/supabase/browser-client.ts.
139: - Server authorization uses auth.getUser(), which validates the JWT.
140: - Browser session reads may use auth.getSession().
141: - Never expose SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY to client code.
142: - Service-role access never replaces application authorization checks.
143: - Ground SQL in schema and policy evidence. Local drafts may use source and
144:   migrations with assumptions recorded; verify the actual schema and policies
145:   on the named target before applying SQL or claiming compatibility. Repository
146:   documentation may lag behind that target.
147: - Every migration, RLS policy, repository, or database API change requires the
148:   Supabase/RLS review gate.
149: 
150: ### Application user ID versus Supabase UID
151: 
152: The users table has two distinct identifiers:
153: 
154: - users.id is the application UUID used by foreign keys.
155: - users.supabase_uid maps the row to auth.uid().
156: 
157: Therefore:
158: 
159: - RLS compares users.supabase_uid with auth.uid()::text.
160: - API routes resolve the application user by supabase_uid.
161: - Application foreign keys use users.id.
185: ## UI interaction
186: 
187: Interactive children inside clickable cards use data-avatar-interactive where
188: applicable. Parent handlers must ignore interactive descendants such as links,
189: buttons, role=button, and data-space-action. Portal menu content must stop
190: pointer, click, and keyboard propagation when needed to prevent accidental room
191: navigation.
192: 
193: ## Test policy
194: 
195: Write only integration and E2E tests. Unit tests are forbidden.
196: 
197: - Integration tests run against real local infrastructure: the local Supabase
198:   database, RLS, Storage, and Realtime, or the real API route against it.
199: - E2E tests drive the real UI with Playwright, using two accounts when the
200:   behavior involves more than one user.
201: - A test that mocks Supabase, a repository, or the code under test is a unit
202:   test and is not allowed, even when it is called integration.
203: - No new unit tests. When a change touches an existing unit test, replace its
204:   behavior with integration or E2E coverage and then delete it. Record the
205:   removal and its replacement in TRACK, or in the handoff outside spec-to-done.
206: - A spec-to-done SPEC's test strategy uses integration and E2E levels only.
207: 
208: ## Verification
209: 
210: Choose evidence proportional to risk:
211: 
212: - documentation-only changes: check affected instructions, links, metadata,
213:   and any applicable document/skill validator;
214: - focused integration or E2E tests for the changed behavior;
215: - regression tests for the reported failure;
216: - typecheck and lint for touched TypeScript;
217: - build when framework boundaries or production output can be affected;
218: - database/RLS checks against the named target when database behavior is claimed;
219: - browser smoke tests for user-visible workflows;
220: - final diff inspection and diff check.
