91: 
92: ## Handoff
93: 
94: Inside spec-to-done, its REPORT format governs the final report. For work
95: outside it, lead with the outcome, then summarize changes, verification, and
96: real remaining limitations or user actions. Small changes need only a short
97: report.
98: 
99: For work involving database or deployment state, report in this order:
100: 
101: 1. Outcome: which affected workflows are usable and which remain unverified.
102: 2. Changes: distinguish application changes, migration/data state on the named
133: ## Supabase, authentication, and RLS
134: 
135: - API routes and server code use createSupabaseServerClient from
136:   src/lib/supabase/server-client.ts.
137: - Client Components use createSupabaseBrowserClient from
138:   src/lib/supabase/browser-client.ts.
139: - Server authorization uses auth.getUser(), which validates the JWT.
140: - Browser session reads may use auth.getSession().
141: - Never expose SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY to client code.
142: - Service-role access never replaces application authorization checks.
143: - Ground SQL in schema and policy evidence. Local drafts may use source and
144:   migrations with assumptions recorded; verify the actual schema and policies
145:   on the named target before applying SQL or claiming compatibility. Repository
146:   documentation may lag behind that target.
147: - Every migration, RLS policy, repository, or database API change requires the
148:   Supabase/RLS review gate.
149: 
150: ### Application user ID versus Supabase UID
151: 
152: The users table has two distinct identifiers:
153: 
154: - users.id is the application UUID used by foreign keys.
155: - users.supabase_uid maps the row to auth.uid().
156: 
157: Therefore:
158: 
159: - RLS compares users.supabase_uid with auth.uid()::text.
160: - API routes resolve the application user by supabase_uid.
161: - Application foreign keys use users.id.
185: ## UI interaction
186: 
187: Interactive children inside clickable cards use data-avatar-interactive where
188: applicable. Parent handlers must ignore interactive descendants such as links,
189: buttons, role=button, and data-space-action. Portal menu content must stop
190: pointer, click, and keyboard propagation when needed to prevent accidental room
191: navigation.
192: 
193: ## Test policy
194: 
195: Write only integration and E2E tests. Unit tests are forbidden.
196: 
197: - Integration tests run against real local infrastructure: the local Supabase
198:   database, RLS, Storage, and Realtime, or the real API route against it.
199: - E2E tests drive the real UI with Playwright, using two accounts when the
200:   behavior involves more than one user.
201: - A test that mocks Supabase, a repository, or the code under test is a unit
202:   test and is not allowed, even when it is called integration.
203: - No new unit tests. When a change touches an existing unit test, replace its
204:   behavior with integration or E2E coverage and then delete it. Record the
205:   removal and its replacement in TRACK, or in the handoff outside spec-to-done.
206: - A spec-to-done SPEC's test strategy uses integration and E2E levels only.
207: 
222: Inside spec-to-done, run checks at this cadence:
223: 
224: - Per task: type-check and lint. New E2E specs are written with the task but
225:   not run yet.
226: - Per block, after its last task and its fix round: type-check, lint, and the
227:   DB integration suite when server code changed. No E2E run per block.
228: - E2E runs only at the end (owner decision, 2026-10-10: the per-task and
229:   per-block runs cost days and froze the machine). Run one Playwright process
230:   at a time, never alongside another heavy job (review, agents running tests).
231: - Final: one run of every new spec of the work item and of the full
232:   regression E2E suite in one browser only (Chrome) with zero retries; no
233:   Edge or Firefox runs (owner decision, 2026-10-10). A test that fails is
234:   analysed and rerun alone; the suite is not repeated.
235: 
236: Use meaningful regression coverage for changed behavior; do not add tests that
237: only mirror trivial edits or that probe failures no real user or realistic
238: failure can cause. After checks pass, rerun or broaden them only for new
239: changes, failures, or unresolved risk. A required runtime check without its
240: prerequisites remains unverified; it does not block unrelated local checks.
241: 
242: Do not weaken assertions or treat skipped critical tests as passing. Mocks do not
243: prove database concurrency, RLS, Realtime delivery, or multi-user behavior.
244: 
245: Common commands:
246: 
247: - npm run dev
248: - npm run type-check

