SPEC.md:108: | FR-007 | Each note carries a revision. Any change to note fields, participants, summary, or transcript, 
including restore and draft application, is accepted only against the current revision; a stale change is rejected 
with a conflict warning showing the newer state; nothing is overwritten silently. | Must | AC-005 |
SPEC.md:109: | FR-008 | Every accepted change to title, meeting date, participants, content, or summary (including 
restore and draft application) creates one immutable version recording who and when. | Must | AC-006 |
SPEC.md:112: | FR-011 | Note editors create, edit, and delete action items (description required, 1–1,000 chars; one 
optional assignee chosen from current company members; optional date-only due date); editors and the current assignee 
toggle completion; an assignee who is not an editor can change nothing but completion; at most 100 items per note. | 
Must | AC-008, AC-009 |
SPEC.md:119: | FR-018 | The draft is transient and carries its source: the note revision and stored-transcript 
identity at generation start. The user edits the summary and accepts, edits, reassigns, or rejects each suggested 
item; "Apply" is accepted only if the note is still at that source revision (otherwise conflict, FR-007), rechecks the 
caller's rights inside the transaction, and atomically writes the summary, appends the accepted items, and creates one 
version, or changes nothing. Suggestions whose description matches an existing item of the note (case- and 
accent-insensitive) arrive unchecked and marked "already exists"; existing items are never modified or removed by a 
draft; Apply is refused if the resulting total exceeds 100 items. | Must | AC-010, AC-027, AC-030 |
SPEC.md:122: | FR-021 | If processing fails (provider error, timeout, refusal, malformed or truncated output, invalid 
key), the note and transcript remain as they were, the user sees what happened with "Try again", and no draft is 
applied. Leaving the page cancels the browser request; the server makes a best-effort abort of the outbound provider 
request and discards the draft; counting follows FR-022 regardless of whether the abort succeeds. Before returning a 
draft, the server rechecks that the caller is still a member of the note's company and the note still exists; 
otherwise the draft is discarded. | Must | AC-016, AC-030 |
SPEC.md:123: | FR-022 | Usage: each provider dispatch counts toward the company's daily limit, whether it succeeds, 
times out, returns unusable output, or the user leaves. A dispatch is refunded only when the provider's documented 
protocol signals rejection before processing — an HTTP authentication/permission error, or a 
rate-limit/overload/unavailable HTTP error status returned before any response body or stream event — as defined per 
adapter in the capability matrix; every other or ambiguous outcome stays counted. Not counted at all: files rejected 
by FR-013, inputs refused by FR-020, failures before dispatch. Admission atomically reserves one unit against the 
limit before dispatch; concurrent requests cannot exceed the limit; each reservation covers exactly one dispatch and 
settles at most once against the day of its admission; a reservation without a recorded outcome (server crash) stays 
counted; only server code settles reservations — no client role can classify, refund, or alter usage. | Must | AC-016, 
AC-028, AC-029 |
SPEC.md:130: | FR-029 | Each note has an immutable company and an immutable author (application `users.id` plus a 
display-name snapshot), set by the server from the creating user at creation. Versions record their editor the same 
way. Deleting a user keeps the notes, versions, and name snapshots (the live reference becomes empty); it never blocks 
the deletion or deletes company notes. | Must | AC-020, AC-023, AC-031 |
SPEC.md:131: | FR-030 | Participants and assignees are stored by application `users.id` with a display-name snapshot. 
A person who is no longer a member of the note's company (company changed, removed, or user deleted) is shown as 
"Former member" (with the snapshot name), keeps their place in the note and its versions, and has no access. If the 
same `users.id` becomes a member of the same company again, their author/participant/assignee rights return. New 
selections accept current members only; saving or restoring may retain former members already present. | Must | AC-021 
|
SPEC.md:132: | FR-031 | Every processing start carries an idempotency key scoped to the authenticated application 
user, company, and operation, and bound to the note id, source note revision, transcript identity, and settings 
revision. A replay with the same key and binding returns the same pending or final result without a new dispatch 
(after re-authorizing the caller); a replay with a different binding is rejected; keys are kept for at least 48 hours, 
across midnight, so a delayed replay never counts again. "Try again" after a failure is a new operation with a new key 
and a new admission. | Must | AC-028 |
